    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d48">

                <pb facs="0060" n="50" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg50"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.927233</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hamilton</hi>)<anchor n="65a"
                        xml:id="d48-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-01-31">January 31, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>We believe it advisable that this Government in some way make known to the
                    Japanese Government its interest in Japan’s mediation of the Thai-French
                    Indochinese controversy. The procedure suggested in one of the attached
                        telegrams<anchor n="66" xml:id="d48-f2"/> is designed to do that in a way
                    which would not expose this Government to the same risk of a rebuff as the
                    procedures previously discussed.<anchor n="67" xml:id="d48-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>M[<hi rendition="#small-caps">axwell</hi>] M. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">amilton</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d48-f1" n="65a" type="source" xml:id="d48-f1-content">
                <p>Addressed to the Secretary of State and the Under Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d48-f2" n="66" xml:id="d48-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 76, February 3, 8 p.m., to the Ambassador in Japan, <ref
                        target="#pg54">p. 54</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d48-f3" n="67" xml:id="d48-f3-content">
                <p>Notation by the Secretary of State: “Only a suggestion to consider: Grew might
                    accompany this cable by copy of my statement of principles of July 1937, so
                    Japan cannot claim not to understand U. S. exact attitude. C. H.” For the
                    Secretary’s statement of July 16, 1937, see <ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_325">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 325</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
